  "nameStory": "The Rugose-chested Sweat Bee gets its name from the distinctively textured surface of its thorax (chest area), which appears wrinkled or ridged - 'rugose' means wrinkled or corrugated in scientific terms. Like all sweat bees, it earned the 'sweat' part of its name because these small bees are attracted to human perspiration, landing on sweaty skin to lap up the salt and moisture they need.",
  "description": "The Rugose-chested Sweat Bee is a small, dark-colored native bee about the size of a small ant or housefly. These compact bees have a distinctly textured, wrinkled-looking chest that sets them apart from their smoother relatives. They're part of the large sweat bee family, which includes some of our most important native pollinators. Unlike honeybees, they're typically solitary insects that nest in the ground rather than forming large colonies.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Rugose chest texture",
      "description": "The thorax (middle body section) has a distinctly wrinkled, ridged surface that feels rough if gently touched, unlike the smoother chest of most other small bees",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "About 6-8mm long, roughly the size of a large ant or small housefly, making it one of the smaller bees you'll encounter",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "Primarily dark brown to black body with minimal metallic sheen, appearing more matte than the iridescent greens and blues of many sweat bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground-nesting behavior",
      "description": "Often seen entering or emerging from small holes in bare soil or sparse vegetation, creating tiny dirt mounds around nest entrances",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flower visiting pattern",
      "description": "Moves quickly between small flowers, spending only seconds at each bloom before buzzing to the next one with rapid, darting flight",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Rugose-chested Sweat Bees in open areas with bare or sparsely vegetated soil where they can excavate their underground nests. They prefer sunny locations like field edges, gardens, trails, and disturbed ground areas throughout New England's lowlands and foothills. You'll often find them near diverse wildflower patches, especially areas with small native blooms like asters, goldenrod, and other late-season flowers.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults begin emerging from underground nests in late spring, with females starting to excavate new burrows in warm, sunny soil.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when adults are most visible visiting flowers for nectar and pollen, with females busy provisioning their underground brood cells.",
    "fall": "Adults continue foraging into early fall on late-blooming flowers like asters and goldenrod before the first hard frosts end their season.",
    "winter": "Overwintering as larvae or pupae deep in underground nest burrows, protected from freezing temperatures by soil insulation."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called 'sweat bees,' these tiny pollinators are actually performing a public service when they land on you - they're helping to regulate your skin's salt levels and are so gentle that most people never even feel their presence, making them nature's tiniest, most polite janitors.",
  "confusionNotes": "The rugose chest texture is the key difference from other small dark sweat bees in the Lasioglossum genus - if you can see wrinkles on the thorax, you've found your species. Remember 'Rugose = Rough and Ridged' to distinguish it from the smoother-chested relatives.",
  "contextualKnowledge": null,
  "compareHints": "Among the many Lasioglossum sweat bees in New England, the Rugose-chested Sweat Bee stands out for its distinctly textured thorax, while relatives like the Deep-blue Sweat Bee show metallic coloration and the White-banded Sweat Bee displays obvious pale stripes. Most other species in this genus have smooth, often shiny chest areas, making the rugose texture a reliable field mark for identification.",
